## Proof

**Claim:** Yes. A continuous function on a closed interval can have an uncountable set of accumulation points of its zeroes.

**Construction.**

Let $C \subseteq [0,1]$ be the Cantor set. Recall three standard facts about $C$:

1. $C$ is **closed** (it is the intersection of the closed sets $C_n$ obtained by repeatedly removing middle thirds).
2. $C$ is **uncountable** (cardinality $|\mathbb{R}|$).
3. $C$ is **perfect**: every point of $C$ is an accumulation point of $C$. (Equivalently $C$ has no isolated points; this follows because every endpoint of a removed interval is a two-sided limit of points of $C$, and every non-endpoint is surrounded by points of $C$ at every stage.)

Define $f:[0,1]\to\mathbb{R}$ by

$$f(x) \;=\; d(x,C) \;=\; \inf\{|x-c| : c\in C\},$$

the distance from $x$ to the Cantor set.

**$f$ is continuous.** For any $x,y\in[0,1]$ and any $c\in C$,

$$d(x,C)\le |x-c|\le |x-y|+|y-c|.$$

Taking the infimum over $c\in C$ gives $d(x,C)\le |x-y|+d(y,C)$, i.e. $d(x,C)-d(y,C)\le|x-y|$. By symmetry, $|f(x)-f(y)|\le|x-y|$. So $f$ is Lipschitz (constant $1$), hence (uniformly) continuous.

**The zero set of $f$ is exactly $C$.** Since $C$ is closed, $d(x,C)=0\iff x\in C$. Thus

$$Z(f)\;=\;\{x\in[0,1]:f(x)=0\}\;=\;C.$$

**The accumulation points of $Z(f)$ form an uncountable set.** The set of accumulation points of $Z(f)=C$ within $[0,1]$ is the derived set $C'$. Since $C$ is perfect, every point of $C$ is an accumulation point of $C$, so $C'=C$. Therefore

$$\{\text{accumulation points of zeroes of }f\}\;=\;C,$$

which is **uncountable**.

**Conclusion.** The continuous function $f(x)=d(x,C)$ on the closed interval $[0,1]$ has the Cantor set $C$ as its set of accumulation points of zeroes, and $C$ is uncountable. Hence a continuous function **can** have an uncountable number of accumulation points of zeroes within a closed interval. $\blacksquare$

